// [ARM memory considerations.note]     search in ssTEA source files for a reference to this file.
//
   In all processors, instructions for methods of accessing an "input" or "output" such as a GPIO
   pin are fundamental to the processor.  This is a mechanism in the mesa electronics that moves a
   bit value from a processor register to a power signal emitted or perceived on external pin of
   the chip the processor lives in.  The actual electrical interaction is outside this discussion,
   the configuration of the GPIO pin within the context of the connections it has is complex.
   The focus here is
            "what is the processor instruction that causes the bit to be moved within the mesa?"
   AND
            "what is happening in the processor while the bit is being moved within the mesa?"

   This topic is a key influence on architecture, design, and implementation of embedded software.
   It is one of the central components of the "mesa view" required for embedded implementations.

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Devices respond to a memory operation; the device appears to the processor to be proper memory.
   Memory-access instructions are used to read and write the device's registers, at the speed at
   which the device operates, not the speed at which memory normally responds.  You may have to wait
   when the device and the processor are not operating in the same clock domain or memory priority.

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   A compiled and linked executable is loaded and run.  It may contain initialized data
   variables within the image, and initialization automatically occurs when the loader writes the
   image of the executable into RAM memory.   Explicit code that initializes variables to a known
   condition may become necessary if a "restart" feature exists. Does every product needs a restart?

//

    the memory will only ever be used as
   this variable and therefore the variable is addressable by a fixed and known address.

   "not static" variables exist only while software runs; the scope of their use is limited by ssHL-language.

   The Arduino model for a build is to compile all files together, all software can see all globals.

      int   myGlobal;                  visible to all, permanent
      static int myPrivate;            visible to all, permanent

      void function(void)              visible to all
      {
         int  myPrivate;               not static, visible and exists only while function runs
         static int myPrivateLocal;    visible only while function runs, permanent
      }
//
   "static" data variables within a function or not, with a nonzero-initializer, will be instantiated
   from the image.   This works well for data structure pointers; does pointer math at compile time.
   Variables outside the scope of a function, with a nonzero-initializer, will be
   instantiated from the image.   Those with no initializer will not have an effect on RAM.
   Static or not, variables with a zero-initializer, may be set to zero by code that zeroes all
   zero-initializer variables.  Stack-based structures initialized to 0 may get free code to do that.


   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   A helpful debug trick when the algorithm looks good but acts bad: verify the memory is writable.
   Certain Host OS variables exist in memory and have a valid value, in memory that is not writable.
   The pointer to memory may be at any alignment (bytes 0,1,2,3 of 32-bit value in memory), but in
   order to not cause a access alignment hard fault, the pointer is always forced to 32-bit aligned.
   The address is forced to the next lowest address on a 32-bit boundary:  the least significant
   two bits of the 32-bit address (0x3).  The address given exists at least partially in 32-bit area.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The result when pointed at memory with permission to write and ability to write: quiet.
        this is the case for a valid pointer to memory with permission and ability to access
   the result when pointed at memory with permission to write but no ability: error reported.
        This is the case where the compiler has placed a data structure in "constant" memory because
        the compiler does not believe any software ever writes to that space.  The compiler does not
        know about the accesses through pointer values that ssTEA makes to that space in memory.
   the result when pointed at memory with no permission to write: hard fault.
   the result when pointed at memory with no permission to read: hard fault
        these are errors by the developer
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   #ifdef MESA_OPTIN_DEBUG_MEMORYACCESS
   CTRICK_Prove_MemoryIsWritable ((unsigned long *) pEvApi, (unsigned char *) __func__);
   void CTRICK_Prove_MemoryIsWritable (unsigned long *p32bits, unsigned char * pFuncName)

// S~S  SimpleSal signal ends  S~S
